an undeclared function called with parentheses is an implicit declaration.

MUTUAL RECURSION COULD NOT BE WRITTEN IN ITS NATURAL ORDER:

    int ev(int n){ if(n) return od(n-1); return 1; }
    int od(int n){ if(n) return ev(n-1); return 0; }
    int main(void){ return ev(10); }

    micro-c: od is not a defined symbol

od is not seen until after ev needs it, and there is no way to say so first --
micro-c has no prototype form that registers a function without defining it.
That is stage 2 conformance corpus row 118, and it is the last of the six
"wrong answer" rows that was actually a compiler limitation rather than stale
corpus data.

C89 6.3.2.2 says a call to an undeclared identifier declares it implicitly as
a function returning int with external linkage. This implements that and
nothing more.

ONLY WHEN A '(' FOLLOWS, which is the guard the function-designator branch
twenty lines above already uses, for exactly this reason. A bare unknown NAME
is still a typo and still an error here, where it is cheap and precise to say
so:

    x = nosuchvar;      nosuchvar is not a defined symbol      (unchanged)
    return nosuchfn(1); Target label FUNCTION_nosuchfn is not valid

The second is a link-time failure out of hex2 rather than a parse error --
later and less precise, which is the same trade C itself makes. Without the
guard a designator used as a VALUE would mint a bogus function on every typo.

The symbol is minted with sym_declare into global_function_list rather than
looked up, so everything downstream -- pending_function_designator, the call
sequence, current_target -- finds it exactly as it would a real forward
declaration. No new path, one new producer of an existing one.

MEASURED, EITHER SIDE:

    stage 2 corpus amd64   419 -> 420 of 426        (row 118 closed)
    difftest amd64         103 pass, 0 fail          unchanged
    difftest aarch64       104 pass, 0 fail          unchanged

The six remaining corpus rows are the documented stale ones: 121, 122, 123 and
352 expect sizeof(int) == 8, and 364 and 368 assume a FILE* indexes as bytes.
None of them is a compiler defect and this patch does not touch them.

--- a/cc_core.c
+++ b/cc_core.c
@@ -1276,6 +1276,41 @@
 		return variable;
 	}
 
+	/* AN UNKNOWN NAME FOLLOWED BY '(' IS AN IMPLICIT FUNCTION DECLARATION.
+	 *
+	 * C89 6.3.2.2: a call to an undeclared identifier declares it as a
+	 * function returning int with external linkage. micro-c rejected it, so
+	 * mutual recursion could not be written in its natural order --
+	 *
+	 *     int ev(int n){ if(n) return od(n-1); return 1; }
+	 *     int od(int n){ if(n) return ev(n-1); return 0; }
+	 *
+	 * -- failed with "od is not a defined symbol" at the first function,
+	 * because od is not seen until the second. That is stage 2 corpus row 118.
+	 *
+	 * ONLY WHEN A '(' FOLLOWS, which is the same guard the function-designator
+	 * branch above uses and for the same reason: a bare unknown NAME is still
+	 * a typo and must still be an error here, where it is cheap to say so.
+	 * With the guard, the only thing this accepts is a call, and a call to a
+	 * name that genuinely does not exist still fails -- at link time, from
+	 * hex2, as "Target label FUNCTION_od is not valid". Later and less
+	 * precise, which is the trade C makes too.
+	 *
+	 * The symbol is minted rather than looked up, so the call path downstream
+	 * finds it exactly as it would a real forward declaration. */
+	if(NULL != global_token)
+	{
+		if(global_token->s[0] == '(')
+		{
+			struct token_list* implicit = sym_declare(s, integer, global_function_list, TLO_FUNCTION);
+			global_function_list = implicit;
+			pending_function_designator = implicit;
+			emit_load_named_immediate(REGISTER_ZERO, "FUNCTION_", s, "implicit function load");
+			current_target = function_pointer;
+			return implicit;
+		}
+	}
+
 	line_error();
 	fputs(s ,stderr);
 	fputs(" is not a defined symbol\n", stderr);
